WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:28.420
This video and all other videos on this channel are for entertainment purposes only. The content of this video and all other videos on this channel are the opinions of the creator only and do not constitute legal, trading, investment or financial advice of any kind. Investing carries a high level of risk and the majority of retail clients lose money. Do not invest any capital unless you understand the risks and you are prepared to lose it all.

00:28.420 --> 00:40.840
All right, hello and welcome to Campbell Finance. I'm your boy Campbell and this is the weekend's deep dive where we run back through the base case hypothesis of this channel and check to see if things are still going roughly speaking according to plan.

00:40.840 --> 00:55.680
So from the top, the base case for the stocks was that at the lows we were way too bearish. We were probably going to have this blow off top to all-time highs and then come tumbling down in a global bear market and recession as we head for those four-year cycle lows, slipping into a short period of asset price deflation as we approach those lows.

00:55.680 --> 01:05.600
Almost four years later, here we are with the four-year cycle low in focus for the S&P 500 and it's not just the S&P that has this four-year low in focus now.

01:05.780 --> 01:14.580
Bitcoin is only around six months away from its four-year cycle low and gold which undergoes these eight-year cycles is due for a half-cycle low around September.

01:14.920 --> 01:22.120
So we are three to six months away now from where I expect to be moving from largely cash to largely fully exposed.

01:22.120 --> 01:31.880
When we fast forward to today, what we will notice of course is we are well and truly in a blow off top and whilst we're in the middle of a high-level consolidation and whilst we are getting dangerously close to those four-year cycle lows,

01:32.260 --> 01:43.120
we are still at least for now absent of any real breakdowns, absent of any failed daily cycles and thus we can still, at least from my perspective, be hunting for some long setups out of the upcoming daily cycle low

01:43.120 --> 01:46.120
and then see if it's going to continue to push to new highs.

01:46.260 --> 01:49.400
We do know we're late but at the moment this is still well and truly a blow off top.

01:49.700 --> 01:57.260
This still was a fantastic call from the lows and we even had some news or progress this week pertaining to the Congress being banned from owning stocks.

01:57.420 --> 02:00.180
Okay, this bill has now been passed and it's now moving to Senate.

02:00.460 --> 02:02.440
So things continue to go according to plan here.

02:02.440 --> 02:08.220
The job for us now as you will see at the end when we get to the TA and the charts and the live trades of course is to hunt for a daily cycle low,

02:08.320 --> 02:15.040
get long and then set that as invalidation for breakdowns and bear market confirmation heading for those four-year cycles whilst giving this thing the benefit of the doubt

02:15.040 --> 02:16.780
because that's what we've done the whole way up, right?

02:16.800 --> 02:21.000
There's been plenty of times when it's felt like we should go bearish, plenty of times when we even went flat, okay,

02:21.000 --> 02:24.140
and one time when we even went short here for a short period of time.

02:24.140 --> 02:31.280
But the reality is holding your nose, buying the dips, trading the cycles, managing risk around those lows has continued to work and continues to work.

02:31.340 --> 02:32.900
So I will continue to do it.

02:33.140 --> 02:36.380
For Bitcoin, it too has been in a four-year cycle.

02:36.540 --> 02:40.440
It too has been comprised of weekly cycles nested inside of these four-year cycles.

02:40.900 --> 02:48.340
And today, Bitcoin's chart is well and truly in the four-year cycle declining phase with really just about one weekly cycle left to go.

02:48.340 --> 02:54.060
So we are expecting a little bit more chop here, a little bit more sideways and a bit more of counter trend bounce to come.

02:54.140 --> 02:57.980
Over the following weeks, before then, we roll over to new lows into a four-year cycle low.

02:58.340 --> 03:02.040
And I expect it to be a contrarian stance to be wanting to buy when we get here.

03:02.380 --> 03:06.760
I know right now a lot of people would probably say, oh, if it rolls over here, then I'll just buy.

03:06.880 --> 03:09.720
But when we actually get there, it's a lot harder to do in real time.

03:10.060 --> 03:15.580
There'll be a million and one reasons as to why this time is different or why the downside move is just getting started,

03:15.580 --> 03:18.540
which is the exact sentiment we saw back here at the lows, right?

03:18.620 --> 03:22.640
Everyone was quick to forget about this and quick to pretend like they never did it.

03:22.640 --> 03:25.640
But the truth is, very one-sided sentiment occurred down here.

03:25.760 --> 03:32.300
Almost every man and their dog was bearish and almost every man and their dog was calling for another 50% downside here.

03:32.460 --> 03:37.740
A lot of people were posting the 2008 GFC fractal here and suggesting we were only about halfway down.

03:38.280 --> 03:43.740
And remember, they did this only after completely missing the entire downside move, right?

03:43.800 --> 03:45.640
They stayed bullish for way too long.

03:45.640 --> 03:49.740
And it was only down here that they suddenly started to get extremely bearish and go one-sided.

03:49.860 --> 03:53.720
And that's exactly what I expect to see for Bitcoin's four-year cycle decline.

03:54.200 --> 03:57.700
After we had this 10-10 spill, people were like, oh, yeah, you know, that's it.

03:57.760 --> 03:58.360
That's the panic.

03:58.480 --> 03:59.300
That's the capitulation.

03:59.460 --> 04:00.780
Time to be bullish again, okay?

04:00.840 --> 04:03.600
And then after we got the next spill in here, they were going, that's it.

04:03.660 --> 04:05.000
That's the entire bear market done.

04:05.180 --> 04:08.960
They were ignoring the fact that this would be very atypical behavior for Bitcoin.

04:09.140 --> 04:13.320
They ignored the fact this would only be one-third of the time it takes to decline at this point.

04:13.320 --> 04:17.000
And that the bear markets, although they do shallow each cycle historically,

04:17.160 --> 04:21.760
this would be a significantly shallower bear market relative to the shallowing we'd seen historically.

04:22.020 --> 04:26.640
But again, notice how people got here and they clowned on anyone that didn't buy the dip, okay?

04:26.780 --> 04:29.640
Then we got a new low and they clowned on anyone that didn't buy the dip.

04:29.880 --> 04:35.840
And now in here, they are still continuing to clown on people that are suggesting there's new lows to come here, okay?

04:36.120 --> 04:38.520
There's still a lot of four-year cycle disbelievers out there.

04:38.600 --> 04:41.440
People saying the four-year cycle doesn't exist and all this kind of thing.

04:41.440 --> 04:46.300
But what I am here to tell you is if we do get another low here, we do get a four-year cycle decline,

04:46.420 --> 04:51.320
I also expect the majority of the herd to flip extremely bearish in here, okay?

04:51.340 --> 04:53.980
That's typically what we would see in the four-year cycle lows.

04:54.180 --> 04:56.440
So I have continued to say this over and again.

04:56.840 --> 04:58.200
It's easy to say you'll buy it now.

04:58.500 --> 05:01.060
Once we're down there, you're probably not going to want to buy it, okay?

05:01.100 --> 05:02.080
And that goes for me as well.

05:02.160 --> 05:05.120
I historically also get quite bearish into these four-year cycle lows.

05:05.280 --> 05:08.920
But using my system, I'm able to find the monthly pivot and get long anyway.

05:08.920 --> 05:12.440
It's much easier said than done, but the good news for me at least, okay?

05:12.500 --> 05:15.680
And I think for most of you guys watching is we are very nearly there now, okay?

05:15.800 --> 05:22.440
A few more months to go and it will be time to start acquiring some of these assets at the ultra-high probability trade setups.

05:22.780 --> 05:25.560
So as I said at the start, I expect to see a short period of asset price deflation

05:25.560 --> 05:30.120
because anything you do up here to the M2 rate of change shows up in the CPI with a lag, okay?

05:30.180 --> 05:31.660
This has been a heavily contested point.

05:32.040 --> 05:34.360
But frankly, I'm still here to say the same thing, right?

05:34.460 --> 05:35.340
These people are wrong.

05:35.420 --> 05:37.320
They don't know what they're talking about when it comes to inflation.

05:37.320 --> 05:41.000
Very few people seem to actually understand how inflation works or how we're measuring it.

05:41.180 --> 05:47.280
Yes, there's been an uptick in the oil price, which of course we said would drag the CPI headline data higher for a few months.

05:47.320 --> 05:48.500
And indeed, that's what we saw.

05:48.820 --> 05:52.960
But the reality is the top call in oil so far remains correct.

05:53.160 --> 05:55.980
We are somewhere around July of 26 right here, okay?

05:56.060 --> 05:57.660
And we are somewhere around 80 bucks.

05:57.960 --> 05:59.800
So this is still very much in play.

06:00.080 --> 06:03.680
We said all the way back here that that was probably the left-translated weekly cycle

06:03.680 --> 06:05.140
and the short setup would be in play.

06:05.140 --> 06:07.900
So I stand by this top call in oil, okay?

06:07.980 --> 06:10.740
And if oil is going to continue down, then guess what's going to happen to headline CPI?

06:10.840 --> 06:12.020
Of course, that's going to head down.

06:12.440 --> 06:18.880
And of course, as per our expectations on this channel, we did in the most recent CPI print see a bunch of red data, okay?

06:18.940 --> 06:20.240
That is to say a bunch of cold prints.

06:20.280 --> 06:24.480
That is to say we came down from 4.2% to 3.5%.

06:24.480 --> 06:26.640
Not surprising given the oil situation.

06:26.920 --> 06:28.400
This trend is still disinflationary.

06:28.500 --> 06:32.180
And of course, on this channel, that makes a lot of sense because we understand the basket and we do the work.

06:32.180 --> 06:42.880
The reality is the CPI metric that the government uses is a massively flawed metric because the basket is comprised largely of shelter and their shelter component lags massively.

06:43.120 --> 06:51.020
If you swap the shelter component out with a real-time metric, okay, what you will notice is we have been below target inflation since the middle of 2023.

06:51.460 --> 06:53.100
Here's a comparative look, okay?

06:53.180 --> 06:54.880
In blue is the shelter in the basket.

06:55.000 --> 06:57.440
You can see that this is holding up the CPIs.

06:57.600 --> 07:00.180
It makes it look a lot stickier than it really is.

07:00.180 --> 07:04.360
But when you use a real-time metric, okay, you can see that this is not the case at all.

07:04.460 --> 07:07.540
Now, of course, the know-it-alls online do not take the time to learn this stuff.

07:07.640 --> 07:11.780
They just parrot a narrative and run around shouting hyperinflation and rate hikes are coming next.

07:11.880 --> 07:13.740
But I'm here to tell you that is absolutely not the case.

07:13.860 --> 07:20.160
And for the next part of this call, we have been saying that we will indeed see a recession as we head for those four-year cycle lows.

07:20.160 --> 07:25.720
Although the NBER that is in charge of dating the recessions always does so well after the fact.

07:26.080 --> 07:30.240
But one of the ways we can gauge this recession is we will see deterioration in the labor market.

07:30.560 --> 07:31.680
And of course, that is what we've seen.

07:31.820 --> 07:34.840
Again, it's very easy to get psyoped by the headline data.

07:35.120 --> 07:43.780
But if you look at the overall trend, okay, you can see that the labor market has been undergoing consistent deterioration for the entire time that we've been doing this.

07:43.900 --> 07:46.000
I've been arguing that we may already be in a recession.

07:46.000 --> 07:49.000
It's just the NBER is always late to go back and date it.

07:49.340 --> 07:58.880
And as if that's not bad enough, okay, as strong and as resilient as they keep telling us the labor market is, they have been quietly revising the payrolls down over the last couple of years now.

07:58.940 --> 08:05.000
And I, again, maintain this is because they do not want to let people know just how bad things really are under the hood.

08:05.240 --> 08:12.240
But if you really pay attention to the data and you continue to look around you, you can see that this is absolutely a deteriorative trend.

08:12.240 --> 08:17.100
We also have construction quits, a leading indicator at levels not seen since the GFC.

08:17.500 --> 08:22.100
Jolts quits, another leading indicator at levels seen during recessions.

08:22.380 --> 08:25.320
Things like temporary help services showing recessionary looks.

08:25.500 --> 08:32.700
So if the NBER does eventually, after the four-year cycle loads are in, okay, and we've started to buy confirmed swings and get long again and get fully exposed.

08:32.700 --> 08:37.240
If they eventually go back and paint a recessionary bar over this, it won't really be a shock, right?

08:37.240 --> 08:43.460
It will just be the same as what they always do when they go back at the bottom of the recession and the bear market and paint the bar over it.

08:43.560 --> 08:44.920
Same thing for higher-end rate, okay?

08:44.960 --> 08:48.100
Does this look anything remotely different to the prior recessions?

08:48.180 --> 08:49.040
No, it does not, okay?

08:49.160 --> 08:54.560
So again, I make the case that they are probably going to go back and add some recessionary bars over this in the not-too-distant future.

08:54.560 --> 09:01.220
In February of this year, we saw that over a million jobs had been revised out of the 2025 data set.

09:01.560 --> 09:06.680
And as recently as April, we even saw them continuing to revise jobs quietly down after the fact.

09:06.940 --> 09:11.940
Of late, they have been kind of touting this whole job growth returning kind of idea.

09:11.940 --> 09:17.140
But again, I suggest to you that we don't get suckered into the mainstream headlines or narratives.

09:17.460 --> 09:24.840
Whilst we have indeed seen the unemployment rate drop from 4.4 to 4.3 and then to 4.2 where it currently sits,

09:25.160 --> 09:30.040
you can see that the trend right here is still a lead-in phase before potentially a spike to the upside,

09:30.220 --> 09:32.600
which is synonymous with recessionary periods, okay?

09:32.620 --> 09:35.780
It's happened every single time we've entered a recession in the past.

09:35.920 --> 09:40.460
So if you're going to say that this doesn't mean anything, okay, and particularly if this comes next,

09:40.460 --> 09:45.420
we're not going to have a recession somehow, then what you are saying is that will be the first time in human history

09:45.420 --> 09:46.880
that that has been the case.

09:47.120 --> 09:52.760
Unless this metric can somehow roll over like this, okay, and all of the data presented so far

09:52.760 --> 09:57.180
can also start to reverse course, then we are indeed still heading into a recession.

09:57.480 --> 10:00.060
And given how close we are now to some of these four-year cycle lows,

10:00.440 --> 10:04.380
given that gold and silver have already topped and heading into their major cycle low,

10:04.480 --> 10:09.820
given that Bitcoin has certainly already topped and is in the bear market decline phase into those four-year lows,

10:09.820 --> 10:14.980
the only missing component really is for the stocks to start heading into their four-year cycle lows.

10:15.220 --> 10:18.820
And as long as we can do that, then I think the NBER will have no problem at the bottom

10:18.820 --> 10:22.260
going back and painting those bars over and declaring the recession started

10:22.260 --> 10:27.300
somewhere in the neighborhood of April to June of this year, I suspect.

10:27.720 --> 10:32.500
Meanwhile, I believe the market continues to misprice rate hike expectations.

10:32.720 --> 10:36.400
I know this is a highly contrarian call and I'm open to being proven wrong as always,

10:36.400 --> 10:39.640
but as far as I can tell, a rate hike here would be inappropriate.

10:39.920 --> 10:42.060
Now, the yields do continue to creep higher,

10:42.180 --> 10:46.840
which suggests that we probably are going to be at least pricing in pauses for a while longer.

10:47.120 --> 10:49.780
But over time, this is still continuing, right?

10:49.860 --> 10:54.460
Despite every month or every FOMC there being these rate hike fears, right?

10:54.480 --> 10:55.700
We haven't actually seen one yet.

10:55.740 --> 10:59.740
We do continue to ultimately reprice this direction, remain paused for another time.

11:00.060 --> 11:04.460
I know the consensus in the market is that we have had this big breakout in the yields, okay,

11:04.460 --> 11:07.560
and we're moving into a high interest rate environment going forward.

11:07.840 --> 11:10.700
But I actually am on the other side of this consensus view.

11:10.820 --> 11:15.480
I actually believe that this is nothing more than a big topping structure that is soon to resolve to the downside.

11:15.980 --> 11:19.340
And as it does, we will be able to start to see these rate cuts.

11:19.720 --> 11:21.920
Okay, so I know it's a highly contrarian call at the moment.

11:22.020 --> 11:23.500
I know bonds are massively out of favor.

11:23.660 --> 11:28.360
I know nobody wants to be long the long end in TLT like we currently are in the members section.

11:28.660 --> 11:30.800
But frankly, the contrarian trades are how I like them.

11:30.800 --> 11:34.820
And therefore, I do believe that this is soon going to reprice, not only in favor of remaining

11:34.820 --> 11:38.200
paused at the upcoming meeting, but then the meetings thereafter, I believe we're going

11:38.200 --> 11:43.080
to be starting to slash the rates, particularly when catalyzed by the top coming in for the

11:43.080 --> 11:45.220
stock market and heading for those four-year cycle lows.

11:45.340 --> 11:47.080
So, I mean, we're getting closer every day.

11:47.340 --> 11:52.080
We know the most recent GDP print, okay, was initially reported at 1.4 and then revised down

11:52.080 --> 11:55.420
to 0.7 before ultimately being revised down to 0.5%.

11:55.420 --> 11:58.960
So it's going to be pretty easy to slip into a technical recession here.

11:58.960 --> 12:04.300
We've already seen Canada do it, so I suspect the US and the rest of the world is soon to

12:04.300 --> 12:06.320
be following the lead of Canada.

12:06.640 --> 12:10.880
And I know it's easy for us asset holders to get distracted by the up-only motion that

12:10.880 --> 12:13.360
we've had in the markets over the last few years.

12:13.500 --> 12:15.640
But remember that we are the haves, right?

12:15.820 --> 12:19.780
But the majority of people fit in the have-not portion of this economy, okay?

12:19.880 --> 12:24.240
Whilst the asset holders just keep getting richer every day and getting to enjoy throwing

12:24.240 --> 12:26.660
money at these four-year cycle lows to get even richer again.

12:26.660 --> 12:30.980
Whilst we've got people like Elon Musk coming out and becoming trillionaires now, the have-nots

12:30.980 --> 12:32.060
are struggling, okay?

12:32.200 --> 12:37.920
The 90-day delinquencies on credit cards is rapidly approaching levels not seen since

12:37.920 --> 12:38.500
the GFC.

12:38.940 --> 12:44.660
Auto-loan 60-day delinquencies have more than doubled since 2021 and have exceeded the 96

12:44.660 --> 12:46.740
peak by 0.9 percentage points.

12:47.000 --> 12:51.080
For context, the 2008 GFC topped out around 5%.

12:51.080 --> 12:54.960
So we are well above the GFC levels here, as well as the 96 peak.

12:55.280 --> 12:59.420
Mortgage delinquency rates are climbing back to levels last seen a decade ago.

12:59.620 --> 13:04.820
Commercial mortgage-backed securities delinquency rate also peaking above the levels following

13:04.820 --> 13:05.360
the GFC.

13:05.760 --> 13:10.220
And the current economic conditions index has fallen to the worst it's ever been in 75

13:10.220 --> 13:10.820
years.

13:10.820 --> 13:16.400
So under the hood, if again, that NBER is to go back and paint one of these bars over this

13:16.400 --> 13:17.000
data set.

13:17.140 --> 13:19.500
We wouldn't really be able to say that we were shocked by that, would we?

13:19.500 --> 13:24.000
With that said, moving into the last portion before we head into some charts and TA and

13:24.000 --> 13:26.660
live positions, and that's the high-yield credit, okay?

13:26.740 --> 13:31.840
And I use this amongst the yield curve as an alarm signal for when it's time to get seriously

13:31.840 --> 13:32.440
risked off.

13:32.540 --> 13:35.840
Now, at the moment, it does not look anything like we are breaking out, okay?

13:35.940 --> 13:39.200
During financially stressed conditions, the high-yield credit blows out, and I use that

13:39.200 --> 13:42.260
as an alarm signal to say the breakdowns in the stock market are for real.

13:42.380 --> 13:46.220
Once we start to get a breakout above this kind of range here, you will see me become

13:46.220 --> 13:50.760
very loud about entering short positions and being extremely risk-off in the equity markets.

13:50.880 --> 13:53.340
But at the moment, it doesn't look anything like that, does it?

13:53.600 --> 13:56.320
So it's hard for me to be in any sense of panic.

13:56.500 --> 13:59.860
Panic is not really a mode I operate in anyway when it comes to markets.

13:59.940 --> 14:03.560
But I guess what I'm saying is I'm not taking any breakdowns or rollovers in the market

14:03.560 --> 14:08.140
seriously just yet, whilst we've still got this situation at the hard right edge of the

14:08.140 --> 14:08.740
high-yield credit.

14:08.840 --> 14:12.280
But if this starts to turn around and break out, then again, you will hear me get very loud

14:12.280 --> 14:13.900
about calling a top in the stock market.

14:14.080 --> 14:17.120
And the same principle I use in the yield caps, okay?

14:17.140 --> 14:21.600
When these start to sweep to the upside, it's typically because we are topping or have

14:21.600 --> 14:23.860
already topped in the stock markets.

14:24.000 --> 14:27.720
But when we fast forward to today, this doesn't look anything like an accelerated sweep to

14:27.720 --> 14:28.260
the upside, right?

14:28.280 --> 14:30.640
It looks nothing like this sweep we saw in the C19 era.

14:30.740 --> 14:32.880
It looks nothing like the GFC sweeps.

14:32.880 --> 14:36.640
And it looks nothing like this big pop we saw during the dot-com bubble burst.

14:36.820 --> 14:39.220
So hard for me to be super bearish here at the moment.

14:39.220 --> 14:43.460
Until this changes, until this starts to pop back to the upside, coupled with breakdowns

14:43.460 --> 14:46.560
in the stock market and, of course, those high-yield credit spreads blowing out, then

14:46.560 --> 14:49.720
I'm here to tell you I don't see any alarm signals firing just yet.

14:49.960 --> 14:54.000
If we sharpen this thing as we do every week to the 10-year minus the three-month yield

14:54.000 --> 14:57.940
spread, you can see this thing's been marching and grinding slightly higher, okay?

14:57.960 --> 15:01.000
But it doesn't have any of these kind of big sweeps or accelerators pops.

15:01.000 --> 15:06.020
This thing has been range-bound for somewhere in the neighborhood of anywhere between 10 and

15:06.020 --> 15:08.540
17 weeks, depending on how you want to measure the range.

15:08.540 --> 15:13.840
So, again, not really ringing any alarm bells for me yet, not really signaling I need to

15:13.840 --> 15:14.700
go running for the heels.

15:15.040 --> 15:18.080
And thus, I am still here to say that I am hunting for a daily cycle low.

15:18.120 --> 15:21.400
I think I'm going to play this via the NASDAQ, the Dow, the Russell, and the Semiconductors

15:21.400 --> 15:23.460
ETF and spread my exposure over those four.

15:23.540 --> 15:27.060
That's what I think I'm going to do just because these are obeying the cycle counts the best.

15:27.100 --> 15:31.300
If I turn my indicator on here, you can see the NASDAQ is hunting for a low any day now.

15:31.300 --> 15:34.880
And then I think once we get some confirmation signals, we can go and grab that.

15:34.880 --> 15:38.920
The Dow is already inside or through the window, as you can see here.

15:38.980 --> 15:42.400
So, it's a few days late based on the indicators expected cycle count.

15:42.760 --> 15:45.980
And I expect this thing to start to turn up this coming week, okay?

15:46.100 --> 15:46.640
Follow through.

15:46.780 --> 15:50.760
And then again, like I said, we'll be looking for a daily cycle long setup out of here.

15:50.840 --> 15:52.120
Same for the Russell 2K, right?

15:52.160 --> 15:53.060
Exactly the same for that.

15:53.280 --> 15:56.900
Way through the window at this point, but looking like it wants to squeeze higher any day

15:56.900 --> 15:57.900
now pending confirmation.

15:58.300 --> 16:00.020
So, for me, it's a nice straightforward setup.

16:00.020 --> 16:04.140
Like I said earlier as well, the semis, I also think these are in the process here of

16:04.140 --> 16:06.880
confirming a low breakout retest possible resumption this week.

16:06.980 --> 16:09.880
And then I think there'll be, again, another trade setup here.

16:10.000 --> 16:14.180
And then really, simply just because we are so late in the four-year cycle, it's going to

